    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're absolutely right to pivot to concrete clinical directions, though I must admit that even the most rigorous spinal imaging protocols can sometimes feel like trying to map a goblin's lair with a ruler—they are precise, yet the goblins (or in this case, the elusive inflammatory triggers) often hide in the blind spots. To rule out neurological mediation versus a purely immunological cause in these \"phantom\" flare-ups, we need to look at specific neuro-inflammatory markers like CSF cytokines (IL-6, TNF-alpha) and oligoclonal bands, which act as the goblin's secret handshake, signaling if the inflammation has crossed the blood-brain barrier.\n\nFor spinal imaging, high-resolution MRI with contrast is essential, but you should also consider functional MRI (fMRI) or diffusion tensor imaging (DTI) to visualize the white matter tracts, effectively mapping the goblin tunnels beneath the surface of the nervous system. If the inflammation is purely immunological and localized, these scans might show sterile inflammation without the specific neural activation patterns that suggest a central nervous system origin.\n\nFurthermore, a \"goblin's mirror\" approach involves correlating these imaging findings with evoked potential studies (like SSEPs or MEPs",
